<%# The toast / banner collision lab page.

    THREE engine partials on one page, because the defect only exists where all
    three are. studio/banners/stack renders the environment bar; the modal host
    is what puts `modal-open` on body and so LIFTS that bar to a pinned
    var(--z-banner) at top 0; layouts/studio/flash puts a toast at top 0 as well.
    Any two of the three and the page cannot exhibit anything at all.

    Every one of them is rendered BY NAME. Nothing here reimplements a toast, a
    banner or a modal — the page only supplies the two controls that put it into
    the state under test, and both are ordinary Alpine bindings onto engine
    behaviour that already exists.

    THE TOAST DELIBERATELY CARRIES A BUTTON. The manager in _flash gives any
    toast with buttons a duration of 0, so it never auto-dismisses. That is what
    makes Dismiss the toast's ONLY exit, and it is the whole difference between
    "a banner covers a toast for four seconds" and "the toast is stuck on this
    page for the rest of the session".

    THE CONTROLS ARE PINNED TO THE BOTTOM, which is not decoration. They have to
    be reachable at a SCROLL OFFSET, because a stacking defect between two pinned
    layers can read correctly at the top of a document and be wrong for every
    reader who scrolled first. A control in normal flow would be scrolled off,
    and Playwright would scroll the page back to reach it — quietly undoing the
    offset the spec came to measure. Pinned, they are clickable at any scroll
    position without moving it. They also sit clear of the toast's own fixed box
    at the top of the viewport, so a toast never covers the control that raises
    the next one.

    The tall filler is what gives the page somewhere to scroll TO.

    THE MODAL HOST RENDERS INSIDE THE x-data SCOPE, not after it, and that is
    load-bearing rather than tidy. Its backdrop lives behind a bare `template
    x-if` reading a `$store` magic, and outside a component scope Alpine leaves
    that template alone: measured here, the store DID take the entry and body DID
    get `modal-open`, while no dialog element was ever created. A page that
    mounted the host as a sibling would report a modal it never rendered, and the
    hit test below would be measuring an empty viewport. The same reason
    /lab/modal_host wraps its own. `main` carries no transform, filter, opacity
    or z-index, so it creates no stacking context and the backdrop still
    composites at var(--z-modal) against the page. %>
<%= render "studio/banners/stack" %>
<%= render "layouts/navbar" %>
<%= render "layouts/studio/flash" %>

<main class="p-8" x-data>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Toast over banner</h1>
  <p data-test="lab-body">toast over banner lab</p>

  <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3"
       style="position: fixed; left: 1rem; bottom: 1rem;">
    <button type="button" data-test="raise-toast" class="btn btn-outline"
            @click="window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('toast', { detail: {
              type: 'notice',
              title: 'Saved',
              message: 'This toast carries a button, so it never auto-dismisses.',
              buttons: [{ label: 'Undo', style: 'primary' }]
            } }))">
      Raise toast
    </button>

    <button type="button" data-test="open-modal" class="btn btn-primary"
            @click="$store.modals.open('lab-card', { title: 'Lab card' })">
      Open modal
    </button>
  </div>

  <div style="height: 4000px"></div>

  <%= render "studio/modals/host" do %>
    <template x-if="$store.modals.current().id === 'lab-card'">
      <div class="p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3" x-text="$store.modals.current().props.title"></h2>
        <button type="button" data-test="card-control" class="btn btn-primary">Card control</button>
      </div>
    </template>
  <% end %>
</main>

